<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Genghisid principle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Genghisid, Chinggisid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Genghisid principle was the belief that only male-line descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> could legitimately hold the title of khan. It shaped politics in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a> and constrained non-Genghisid rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, who ruled through puppet khans and used the title amir in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>. The concept is also used to compare how other dynasties, such as the Zand, sought legitimacy.</p></div>
